# Zephyr split Link Layer (ULL/LLL) lower layer for the EFR32BG22 radio.
#
# Runs on BG22 silicon (docs/radio-tables.md §7.84); see docs/zephyr-ll-port.md.
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

config BT_LLL_VENDOR_EFR32
	bool "Open EFR32BG22 lower link layer for Zephyr's split controller"
	depends on BT_LL_SW_SPLIT
	depends on SOC_SERIES_EFR32BG22 || SOC_SERIES_BGM22
	default y
	# Same feature selection as Zephyr's OpenISA LLL (subsys/bluetooth/controller/
	# Kconfig.ll_sw_split, BT_LLL_VENDOR_OPENISA): legacy advertising/scanning, one-PHY connections,
	# AES and software TIFS. Nothing here is a claim about what the radio can do. Its AES (the
	# session-key ECB and the link-layer CCM) runs on the RADIOAES accelerator (crypto/).
	select BT_HAS_HCI_VS
	# The LLL context and radio jobs run at a higher meta-IRQ priority than the ULL worker
	# (hal/ll_threads.c), so the controller's LLL and ULL priorities are settable and distinct.
	select BT_CTLR_ULL_LLL_PRIO_SUPPORT
	# The ticker, launch and QEMU TIFS wake-ups are scheduler clock events, and the
	# ticker count is derived from the scheduler clock's logical timebase.
	select CLOCK_EVENT_SCHED
	select BT_CTLR_ENTROPY_SUPPORT
	select BT_CTLR_CRYPTO_SUPPORT
	select BT_CTLR_LE_ENC_SUPPORT if BT_CTLR_CRYPTO_SUPPORT && !BT_CTLR_DATA_LENGTH_CLEAR
	select BT_CTLR_PRIVACY_SUPPORT if BT_CTLR_CRYPTO_SUPPORT
	select BT_CTLR_PHY_UPDATE_SUPPORT
	select BT_CTLR_EXT_REJ_IND_SUPPORT
	select BT_TICKER_REMAINDER_SUPPORT
	select BT_TICKER_UPDATE if BT_BROADCASTER || BT_CONN
	select EFR32_RADIOAES
	# TX/RX launch uses generic deadline-event latches to atomically retain an
	# IRQ publication while its persistent consumer is running.
	select SCHED_DEADLINE
	help
	  Out-of-tree lower link layer ("vendor") for subsys/bluetooth/controller/ll_sw. The LLL
	  sources are derived at configure time from Zephyr's OpenISA LLL (Apache-2.0); only the
	  hardware abstraction in this module is new. Replaces the Silicon Labs binary controller
	  (BT_SILABS_EFR32); the two are mutually exclusive.

config EFR32_RADIOAES
	bool "AES-ECB, AES-CMAC and AES-CCM on the RADIOAES accelerator"
	depends on SOC_SERIES_EFR32BG22 || SOC_SERIES_BGM22
	help
	  Driver for the BG22's RADIOAES block (a BA411E AES engine behind a scatter-gather DMA):
	  crypto/radioaes.c, include/hal/efr32/radioaes.h. Every register, bit and descriptor field
	  is cited there to the reference manual or to Silicon Labs' Zlib-licensed sources in
	  hal_silabs (docs/crypto.md). Synchronous; callers of any priority may preempt each other.

config EFR32_RADIOAES_PSA
	bool "PSA Crypto transparent driver on RADIOAES"
	default y
	depends on EFR32_RADIOAES && MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_C
	depends on TF_PSA_CRYPTO_CONFIG_FILE = "config-tf-psa-crypto.h"
	help
	  Routes PSA Crypto's one-shot AES-ECB (psa_cipher_encrypt/decrypt), AES-CMAC
	  (psa_mac_compute) and AES-CCM (psa_aead_encrypt/decrypt) to RADIOAES, and builds no
	  software AES for them. The TF-PSA-Crypto dispatch that calls the driver is derived from
	  vendor/tf-psa-crypto at configure time (crypto/CMakeLists.txt). Other AES modes, and
	  multi-part operations, are not accelerated.

	  An application using this module sources Kconfig.defaults from its own Kconfig before
	  Kconfig.zephyr (see that file); configuration fails otherwise.

config EFR32_RADIOAES_PROBE
	bool "TEST ONLY: record the accelerator's busy time"
	depends on EFR32_RADIOAES
	help
	  Stores the CPU cycles (DWT CYCCNT, which the test enables) from each operation's start
	  command to the DMA going idle in efr32_radioaes_probe_cycles: the hardware time that the
	  QEMU model reproduces (tests/crypto-port).

if BT_LLL_VENDOR_EFR32

config EFR32_RADIO_STUB_REGISTERS
	bool "Do not touch radio registers (all radio_* hooks are TODO stubs)"
	default y
	help
	  The radio register map is not public (docs/radio-tables.md). While set, the radio HAL only
	  keeps software state and asserts when the controller tries to put a packet on the air.

config EFR32_RADIO_ULL_STACK_SIZE
	int "Upper link layer worker thread stack size"
	default 640
	help
	  Stack of the persistent thread that runs the ticker and ULL mayflies
	  (hal/ll_threads.c), work Zephyr's split controller otherwise runs in a
	  software interrupt. In the QEMU active-scan workload (dual machine, 90 s)
	  this context peaked at 464 bytes (CONFIG_INIT_STACKS high-water mark).
	  A held, encrypted silicon connection peaks at 648 (docs/radio-tables.md
	  7.84), more than this default: a connection image sizes it from that
	  figure (the product: prj.conf). No code on the link-layer threads
	  executes an FP instruction (7.86), so an interrupt adds a basic
	  exception frame (32 bytes plus at most 4 of alignment), not an FP one.
	  LLL prepares run in the LLL context, not inline here. It runs
	  at the ULL meta-IRQ priority (EFR32_ULL_THREAD_PRIO), below the LLL and
	  the radio's persistent jobs.

config EFR32_RADIO_LLL_STACK_SIZE
	int "Lower link layer thread stack size"
	default 768
	help
	  Stack of the separate persistent LLL mayfly thread, which runs at the
	  LLL meta-IRQ priority with the radio's persistent jobs, above the ULL
	  worker. It runs the LLL prepares (lll_scan_prepare(), lll_adv_prepare()):
	  in the QEMU active-scan workload (dual machine, 90 s) it peaked at 636
	  bytes (CONFIG_INIT_STACKS high-water mark; the advertiser 524); a held,
	  encrypted silicon connection 748 (docs/radio-tables.md 7.84). An interrupt
	  adds a basic exception frame (36 bytes, see EFR32_RADIO_ULL_STACK_SIZE).
	  They are resource bounds, not radio timing policy.

config EFR32_RADIO_LAUNCH_STACK_SIZE
	int "Persistent radio TX/RX launch job stack size (default for both)"
	default 448
	help
	  TX and RX launch jobs only wait for a coalescing event latch, claim their generation,
	  and perform the bounded radio arm write. They deliberately do not invoke
	  controller callbacks. Keep their stacks separate from the
	  completion job so its callback budget does not reserve RAM for both launch
	  paths. The default of EFR32_RADIO_TX_LAUNCH_STACK_SIZE and
	  EFR32_RADIO_RX_LAUNCH_STACK_SIZE, which size the two jobs separately.

	  Exception frames: the launch paths (the jobs, the kernel and the scheduler clock
	  service with its BURTC and TIMER backends) execute no FP instruction, so with
	  FPU_SHARING (lazy stacking, FPCCR.ASPEN|LSPEN) CONTROL.FPCA stays clear on these
	  threads and an interrupt pushes a basic frame, 32 bytes plus at most 4 of alignment,
	  not the 104-byte extended one (docs/radio-tables.md 7.86, from the product image's
	  disassembly).

config EFR32_RADIO_TX_LAUNCH_STACK_SIZE
	int "Persistent radio TX launch job stack size"
	default EFR32_RADIO_LAUNCH_STACK_SIZE
	help
	  Silicon high-water marks in held connections (docs/radio-tables.md 7.84): 208 bytes
	  unencrypted, 428 encrypted (the arm path through the counter driver). Size it at least
	  the measured peak plus one basic exception frame (36 bytes, see
	  EFR32_RADIO_LAUNCH_STACK_SIZE).

config EFR32_RADIO_RX_LAUNCH_STACK_SIZE
	int "Persistent radio RX launch job stack size"
	default EFR32_RADIO_LAUNCH_STACK_SIZE
	help
	  High-water marks in held connections: 208 bytes unencrypted and 368 encrypted on silicon
	  (docs/radio-tables.md 7.84), 372 in the QEMU product run (7.86). Size it at least the
	  measured peak plus one basic exception frame (36 bytes, see
	  EFR32_RADIO_LAUNCH_STACK_SIZE).

config EFR32_RADIO_COMPLETION_STACK_SIZE
	int "Persistent radio completion job stack size"
	default 768
	help
	  FRC completion work invokes the controller callback after leaving the
	  interrupt context, so it retains a larger, independent stack budget.
	  Real EFR32 hardware also enters the Silicon Labs counter driver's
	  absolute-deadline arm path while this job waits; 512 bytes overflowed
	  there, while QEMU's simpler clock path did not expose the requirement.

config EFR32_RADIO_BRINGUP_TRACE
	bool "Print one-shot radio bring-up checkpoints"
	default n
	help
	  Print the first observed PHY, packet framing, TX FIFO, scheduler launch,
	  and TXEN checkpoints. This is intended for initial silicon bring-up only:
	  printk timing is not part of the controller contract and production and
	  simulator regression images should leave it disabled.

config EFR32_RADIO_RX_CHANNEL_STATS
	bool "Count received frames per channel and CRC result"
	default n
	help
	  Diagnostic: efr32_radio_rx_channel_stats[channel][crc_ok] counts every
	  hardware RXDONE by Bluetooth channel index (0-39) and CRC result, for a
	  debugger to read. No printing, no effect on timing beyond one increment
	  per frame.

config EFR32_RADIO_QEMU_RX_DESCRIPTOR
	bool "Enable the QEMU-only synthetic RX descriptor consumer"
	default n
	help
	  Enable the opt-in simulator seam that consumes BUFC channel-2 records
	  (class 6/7), copies a bounded PDU from the channel-1 FIFO, and then
	  allows the existing FRC completion callback to run. The normal EFR32
	  hardware build must leave this disabled: the vendor descriptor producer,
	  record validity, and RXDONE ordering are not established on silicon.
		This option is intended only for an explicitly configured in-tree QEMU
		test image.

config EFR32_RADIO_QEMU_TIFS
	bool "Enable QEMU-only scheduler-owned TIFS turn-around"
	default n
	help
		Enable the opt-in simulator seam that schedules the OpenISA TX-to-RX
		turn-around and no-response timeout through persistent Zephyr jobs. The
		transition is armed only after a controller-observed TX completion and
		is cancelled by radio reset, teardown, or event timer stop. It is not a
		model of the EFR32 PROTIMER/RAC TIFS sequencer: leave this disabled for
		hardware builds because packet-end capture, warm-up timing, and HCTO
		semantics remain unproven on silicon. Its persistent event latch uses the
		vendor LLL's generic deadline scheduling support; deadline ordering is not
		a hardware timing claim.

config EFR32_RADIO_CONN_STAGED
	bool "Stage connection replies and scan responses before the frame ends"
	default y
	depends on !EFR32_RADIO_QEMU_TIFS
	help
	  An advertiser's scan response is staged the same way
	  (lll/lll_adv_stage.c): the sequencer answers a SCAN_REQ for it.

	  Before each receive in a connection event the link layer works out
	  both replies it can give (lll/lll_conn_stage.c) and the HAL hands
	  them, encrypted when the link is, to the radio sequencer, which
	  decides at the frame's end by the link layer's own rules
	  (acknowledgement, new data, MD, CRC errors) and turns around at
	  T_IFS without the M33; the LLL callback then commits the same
	  outcome. Without it the turnaround waits for the M33's callback,
	  whose path is longer than T_IFS allows on silicon
	  (docs/radio-tables.md 7.80, 7.82). The QEMU-only TIFS seam keeps
	  the callback-driven turnaround.

config EFR32_RADIO_ADMISSION
	bool "Admit radio work against its execution budgets"
	default y if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_SILICON
	select SCHED_DEADLINE_ADMISSION
	help
	  Declare the worst-case execution time of each radio job and of the
	  LLL and ULL contexts (k_deadline_event_budget_set(), per publication
	  for the completion job), admit every timed release when it is armed
	  (k_deadline_event_admit_plan()) and let the kernel check every other
	  release when it is posted. An infeasible release is reported as
	  K_DEADLINE_MISS_UNSAT and shuts the radio down like a missed deadline
	  (hal/radio.c).

	  Every start-by deadline is derived from the Bluetooth Core
	  Specification and the measured times below
	  (include/hal/efr32/ll_deadlines.h, docs/radio-tables.md 7.77).

	  On by default with silicon's times, where the advertising,
	  scanning, initiating and connection job sets are feasible: launches
	  are timed by the sequencer and connection replies, scan responses,
	  scan requests and CONNECT_INDs are staged (EFR32_RADIO_CONN_STAGED,
	  docs/radio-tables.md 7.82, 7.87).
	  Off by default with the QEMU model's: there the work the M33 still
	  does in a turnaround is infeasible by analysis: the model's longest
	  interrupt, 72.4 us, exceeds the ADV_IND completion's window (46.6 us
	  after its post) and the QEMU TIFS turnaround's, so admission would
	  stop the radio at the first one. Every start is still checked at
	  run time.

choice EFR32_RADIO_TIMING
	prompt "Platform the measured times come from"
	default EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_SILICON
	help
	  Every budget and path below is measured on the platform that runs
	  the image. QEMU (vendor/qemu, per-core clocks, the M33 at a flat
	  1.84 cycles per instruction) runs this firmware's paths at other
	  speeds than silicon, slower for most (docs/radio-tables.md 7.77), so
	  its images declare its own measurements.

config EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_SILICON
	bool "Silicon (BG22)"

config EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	bool "The QEMU model"

endchoice

# Measured times. Silicon: BGM220 explorer kit (BG22), stage 3 (tests/radio-port, hardware
# configuration) with a host actively scanning, admission on, maxima over 120 s of steady state
# (after zeroing them 20 s after boot) unless stated. QEMU: build/qemu-new, efr32bg22-dual,
# -icount shift=3, admission on, 120 s wall (280-880 s virtual) of each pair: QEMU-configuration
# advertiser and active scanner, hardware-configuration advertiser and scanner, and the mixed
# pair. Runs are CONFIG_SCHED_DEADLINE_STATS (one k_deadline_event_wait() return to the next
# call, in scheduler-clock cycles at 33554432 Hz), paths inside a run are scheduler-clock
# timestamps from the run's start (scratch probes, not committed). Budgets are the longest run
# seen and paths the longest path seen; nothing is added to them.

config EFR32_RADIO_WCET_TX_LAUNCH_NS
	int "TX launch job budget (ns)"
	default 29000 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	default 29500
	help
	  Longest run of the TX launch job, its TXEN hold included. Silicon
	  27.9 us (29.5 us with a longer hold); QEMU 29.0 us.

config EFR32_RADIO_TX_LAUNCH_PRE_NS
	int "TX launch: start to the sequencer post (ns)"
	default 16600 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	default 20400
	help
	  Longest path from the TX launch job's start to posting TXEN to the
	  sequencer (TX FIFO publish, pre-TX handshake, mapping the instant,
	  the mailbox store; docs/radio-tables.md 7.82): silicon 20.4 us
	  (685 cycles, 742 launches, stage-3 advertiser with a host scanning),
	  QEMU 14.4 us (the 7.77 figure, 16.6 us, is kept: it is longer).

config EFR32_RADIO_SEQ_TIMED_WAKE_NS
	int "Timed command: post to the sequencer's first poll (ns)"
	default 3021 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	default 1823
	help
	  Longest time from the M33's RDMAILBOX1 store to the sequencer's
	  first TIMER0 poll of a timed command (TIMER0 counts the M33 reads
	  after its store and the sequencer at its first poll;
	  docs/radio-tables.md 7.82): silicon 136 counts at 76.8 MHz,
	  1.77 us (742 launches, 7.82); 140 counts, 1.82 us, in a held
	  encrypted connection (7.84, the default); QEMU 116 counts at
	  38.4 MHz, 3.02 us.

config EFR32_RADIO_SEQ_TIMED_HOLD_LATE_NS
	int "Timed command: instant to the command store (ns)"
	default 2214 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	default 1576
	help
	  Longest time from a timed command's instant to the sequencer's
	  TIMER0 read right after its TXEN or SWRXEN store
	  (docs/radio-tables.md 7.82): silicon 111 counts at 76.8 MHz,
	  1.45 us (742 launches, 7.82), 121 counts, 1.58 us, in a held
	  encrypted connection (7.84, the default; 76-86 counts in a
	  200-launch PRS run, the
	  RAC leaving OFF 0.9 us before that read); QEMU 85 counts at
	  38.4 MHz, 2.21 us.

config EFR32_RADIO_SPIN_EXIT_NS
	int "Not-before wait exit to job start (ns)"
	default 36300 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	default 5800
	help
	  Longest time from a TX launch's not-before bound to its start when
	  it was released before the bound (k_deadline_event_post_at()):
	  silicon 5.8 us, QEMU 36.3 us.

config EFR32_RADIO_RX_LAUNCH_PRE_NS
	int "RX launch: start to the sequencer post (ns)"
	default 10900 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	default 15710
	help
	  Longest path from a held RX launch's start (a Peripheral's receive
	  window) to posting SWRXEN to the sequencer, the instant's mapping
	  included (as the TX launch's PRE): silicon 15.71 us (527 cycles,
	  a held connection, 7.84; 15.2 us in 7.82's
	  connection attempts from a host Central, 7.82), QEMU 10.9 us.

config EFR32_RADIO_RX_READY_NS
	int "SWRXEN to RXSEARCH (ns)"
	default 167630 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	default 166760
	help
	  RX ready delay: from SWRXEN with the RAC in OFF to the receiver
	  searching (RXWARM, whose handler powers and tunes the synthesizer
	  and the receive chain, then RXSEARCH on its acknowledgement).
	  Silicon: PRS RACL.ACTIVE rise to RACL.RX rise into TIMER1 at
	  76.8 MHz, 166.55-166.76 us (n = 899, hardware scanner); QEMU
	  166.01-167.63 us (434 windows, RAC state trace),
	  docs/radio-tables.md 7.80. The longest is taken.

config EFR32_RADIO_TX_READY_NS
	int "TXEN to the first preamble bit (ns)"
	default 87080 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	default 91650
	help
	  TX ready delay: silicon RACL.ACTIVE rise (TXEN) to RACL.TX rise
	  91.0 us, TX entry to the first preamble bit 40.65 - 40 = 0.65 us
	  (SYNCSENT 40.6-40.7 us after TX entry less the 40 us preamble and
	  access address), docs/radio-tables.md 7.72. QEMU: TXEN to TX entry
	  85.65-86.43 us (2004 launches, RAC trace) + 0.65 us = 87.08 us.

config EFR32_RADIO_RX_EOF_TO_EXIT_NS
	int "Received frame's EOF to the RAC leaving RXFRAME (ns)"
	default 3300
	help
	  Silicon: MODEMH.EOF to the RACL.RX fall 3.2-3.4 us (PRS into
	  TIMER1, docs/radio-tables.md 7.72); the QEMU model uses 3.3 us.
	  RAC.STIMER counts from that exit, so the HAL dates a received
	  frame's end from it.

config EFR32_RADIO_RX_EOF_TO_EXIT_NS_MAX
	int "Received frame's EOF to the RAC leaving RXFRAME, longest (ns)"
	default 3400
	help
	  The longest of the same interval, 3.4 us on silicon (7.72): RXDONE
	  is raised at the EOF, so the RX completion interrupt waits at most
	  this for the RAC to leave RXFRAME before dating the frame.

