menuconfig EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR
	bool "Amateur-radio operation at run time (US 47 CFR Part 97)"
	depends on SCHED_DEADLINE_MISS_CUSTOM
	depends on BT_CTLR_ADVANCED_FEATURES
	select BT_CTLR_USER_EXT
	# 47 CFR 97.313(a): the operator sets the station's power (efr32_amateur_tx_power_set()).
	select EFR32_RADIO_TX_POWER
	select BT_CTLR_LE_ENC_POLICY if BT_CTLR_LE_ENC
	help
	  Lets the application switch the radio, at run time and with a
	  licensee's call sign, from the unlicensed Bluetooth rules (Part 15,
	  2402-2480 MHz) to amateur rules: the carrier then follows a channel
	  plan inside the 13 cm amateur band (2300-2310 and 2390-2450 MHz,
	  47 CFR 97.301(a)) and the radio's measured synthesizer range,
	  link-layer encryption is refused (97.113(a)(4)), and the call sign is
	  sent in Morse code by on-off keying the carrier at least every 10
	  minutes during a communication and at its end (97.119). Off, nothing
	  of it is compiled. The radio stays under Part 15 until
	  efr32_amateur_enable() (include/hal/efr32/amateur.h). Only for
	  licensed amateur stations; see docs/amateur-radio.md.

if EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR

config EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_DATA
	bool "Identify by a data emission too (BLE-ASI; UNCONFIRMED legal basis)"
	help
	  Each station identification also sends (or, with
	  EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_CW off, sends only) the call sign as BLE
	  Amateur Station Identification v1 (docs/ble-amateur-station-id.md):
	  a dedicated non-connectable, non-scannable ADV_NONCONN_IND (Core
	  v6.1 Vol 6 Part B 2.3.1.3) whose only AD structure is Service Data
	  with the BLE-ASI UUID, a header (FINAL on the end-of-communication
	  identification) and the call sign in ASCII, on each advertising
	  channel of the active plan. It is the identification job's own
	  frame, in its reserved radio slot, independent of the
	  application's advertising; about 1 ms on air, so it also fits
	  between connection events. Same schedule, admission and safe
	  shutdown as the CW identification (docs/amateur-radio.md
	  section 5).

	  LEGAL CAVEAT, not confirmed: 47 CFR 97.119(b)(3) allows
	  identification "by a RTTY emission using a specified digital code
	  when all or part of the communications are transmitted by a RTTY or
	  data emission", and 97.309(a)(3) specifies ASCII (ITU-T T.50, IA5).
	  This frame is a data emission (F1D, GFSK, about 1 MHz wide), not a
	  narrow-band RTTY one, and its ASCII octets are carried in Bluetooth
	  framing (preamble, access address, header, UUID, CRC, whitening:
	  publicly documented, cf. 97.309(a)(4)). Whether it satisfies
	  97.119(b)(3) is an open question for the licensee to settle (for
	  example with the ARRL) before relying on it. Until then keep
	  EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_CW on.

config EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_CW
	bool "Identify in Morse code (CW) as well" if EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_DATA
	default y
	help
	  The call sign in Morse code by on-off keying the carrier, 47 CFR
	  97.119(b)(1): the default identification, always on unless the data
	  identification is chosen. With both, each identification sends the
	  data frames and then the Morse frame in one reserved slot (the radio
	  is busy for the Morse frame's 4.3 s). Turning it off leaves only
	  the data identification, whose legal basis is unconfirmed.

choice EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_DATA_ADDRESS
	prompt "Data identification's device address"
	depends on EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_DATA
	default EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_DATA_ADDRESS_OWN

config EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_DATA_ADDRESS_OWN
	bool "An address used only for identification"
	help
	  A static random address drawn when amateur rules are enabled and
	  used only by identification frames (BLE-ASI 6), so receivers that
	  cache names by address do not mix the identification with the
	  application's advertising.

config EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_DATA_ADDRESS_STATION
	bool "The station's usual address"
	help
	  The controller's public address, or else its random (identity)
	  address, as the application's advertising uses.

endchoice

config EFR32_RADIO_WCET_AMATEUR_ID_COMPLETION_NS
	int "Data identification: completion callback of one PDU (ns)"
	depends on EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_DATA
	default 157700 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU && EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_CW
	default 36400 if EFR32_RADIO_TIMING_QEMU
	default 184700 if EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_CW
	default 49500
	help
	  The identification's callback in the completion of one of its PDUs:
	  the next advertising channel's PDU tuned, staged and launched, or
	  after the last one the radio returned to its Bluetooth configuration
	  (with EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_CW, the Morse frame set up and
	  launched). The completion's budget is this plus the measured
	  completion of a transmit (EFR32_RADIO_WCET_COMPLETION_TX_NS, which
	  already includes an LLL callback of its own, so the sum bounds it
	  from above). Its start-by deadline is the advertising PDU spacing,
	  10 ms after the previous TXEN (Core v6.1 Vol 6 Part B 4.4.2.1).
	  Measured (EFR32_RADIO_AMATEUR_ID_PROBE, docs/radio-tables.md 7.85),
	  longest of 12 (6 with CW) identifications: silicon 49.4 us, 184.6 us
	  with the Morse launch; QEMU 36.4 us, 157.6 us.

